---
/*
  About — bio, the community-organizing to faculty arc, teaching, link to
  the CV page, consulting (RCRC). Per the sitemap (revised 2026-07-27: the
  CV is an HTML page at /cv, not a PDF).

  Every date, title, and organization here comes from Jackson or a verified
  source recorded in AGENTS.md — nothing is inferred. Full-page line edit
  with Jackson 2026-07-28: GCF role now leads and the title question is
  settled ("instructor," not "research faculty"); Bio paragraph 3 rebuilt
  on a means-to-end pattern (bio-research session, modeled on Sarah
  Williams / MIT); MCSC board dates corrected (board July 2023, after the
  ED term — supersedes the July 2021 answer); SVS engagement dateless in
  prose (2022 on the CV); the CAP whitepaper generalized to "published work
  and invited presentations in national forums"; Teaching expanded from the
  Fall 2025 / Spring 2026 syllabi (capstone is 484R per Jackson, though the
  syllabus header says 484); Bozeman line removed from the Bio. RCRC
  "partner" and "consulting arm" phrasing is Jackson's (reconfirmed
  2026-07-28; independence line considered and declined).

  2026-07-31, Jackson's call: the Salmon Valley paragraph STAYS but the mine
  is no longer named. It previously read "tied to the Idaho Cobalt
  Operations"; naming the asset identifies the operator in one search, which
  is exactly what the (now withdrawn) Salmon Valley project page was written
  to avoid. This aligns About with that page's generic framing. The CV still
  names the company outright and that divergence remains deliberate — do not
  reconcile them. Context: the Salmon Valley project entry was withdrawn from
  the site the same day (no tangible deliverable, his criterion); About is
  now the only page on the site that mentions the engagement.

  2026-07-31, later: FUNDED RESEARCH SECTION ADDED, his pick of three drafted
  treatments. It closes a measured gap — the site carried 346 words on Home
  and 739 on About and named not one of his four sponsored awards, so the
  only page saying he holds federal money was /cv, which is not in the nav
  and sits behind a link most of the way down this page. Two of his four
  stated audiences, agency program managers and university PIs, read current
  awards as the first credibility signal.

  WHY IT IS HERE AND NOT ON HOME: the ASSURE award is unmanned-aircraft
  training, and UAS and LiDAR appear NOWHERE on Home by standing rule. About
  already names both, so this placement costs no settled decision.

  THE COPY DELIBERATELY DOES NOT COUNT THE AWARDS OR CALL THEM ACTIVE. All
  four were active as this was written, but ASSUREd SAFE ends 30 August 2026,
  so "four active awards" would have been false within a month. Sponsors and
  substance are durable; counts and status are not. Keep it that way.

  NO DOLLAR AMOUNTS, matching the CV and the resume. His decision, and the
  reason holds here too: he is one of four PIs on CORE-CM and ASSUREd SAFE is
  a subaward, so a single figure misleads in both directions.
*/
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import { site } from '../site';
import headshot from '../assets/images/jackson-rose-headshot.jpg';
import yellowstone from '../assets/images/jackson-rose-yellowstone.jpg';
---

<Base
	title="About"
	description="Jackson Rose is Assistant Director and Operations Manager of the MSU Geospatial Core Facility, an instructor in Earth Sciences at Montana State University, and a consultant on the community dimensions of energy and extractive development."
>
	<h1 class="text-4xl">About</h1>
	<p class="mt-3 text-sm text-muted">{site.roles}</p>

	<section class="mt-10">
		<h2 class="text-3xl">Bio</h2>
		{/* 2026-08-13: studio headshot added beside Bio, his pick of two
		    rendered placements. Credit "MSU photo by Colter Peterson" comes
		    from the file's own embedded Artist/dc:creator fields
		    (CP20230227_Jackson Rose-002.JPG, (c)2023 Montana State
		    University). Distinct on purpose from Home's casual outdoor
		    portrait — the two pages carry different registers. */}
		<figure class="mt-4 sm:float-right sm:ml-6 sm:mb-2 sm:w-52">
			<Image
				src={headshot}
				alt="Studio portrait of Jackson Rose smiling in a dark gray sweater against a gray backdrop."
				widths={[320, 640]}
				sizes="(min-width: 640px) 13rem, calc(100vw - 3rem)"
				class="w-full"
			/>
			<figcaption class="mt-2 text-sm text-muted">
				MSU photo by Colter Peterson.
			</figcaption>
		</figure>
		<p class="mt-4">
			Jackson Rose is Assistant Director and Operations Manager of the
			Montana State University Geospatial Core Facility, which provides
			professional GIS services to the campus research community and
			external collaborators, and an instructor in MSU's Department of
			Earth Sciences.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4">
			His research and consulting focus on the economic and social
			dimensions of extractive and energy-sector development: how mines
			and energy projects change the fiscal ground under rural
			communities, and how communities can negotiate durable benefit
			from them. For nearly a decade his work has centered on community
			benefits agreements, from a master's thesis on non-regulatory
			agreements at modern mineral mines, to CBA engagements in Montana
			and Idaho, to published work and invited presentations in national
			forums on mining reform and community benefits.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4">
			He pairs that work with professional GIS practice, using spatial
			and statistical analysis (Python, ArcGIS Pro, open-source GIS),
			geospatial data management, and UAS and LiDAR collection to put
			concrete numbers and maps under the questions rural communities
			face about energy and extractive development. He is a Certified
			GIS Professional and holds an FAA Part 107 remote pilot
			certificate.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="mt-12 border-t border-line pt-8">
		<h2 class="text-3xl">Background</h2>
		<p class="mt-4">
			Before joining MSU's faculty, Jackson ran a community organization
			at the center of negotiations over a controversial copper mining
			project in central Montana. From 2021 to 2023 he was Executive
			Director of the Meagher County Stewardship Council, a grassroots
			501(c)(3) in White Sulphur Springs, where he helped lead
			negotiations with an international mining company over a community
			benefits agreement tied to the Black Butte Copper Project. He has
			served on the Council's board since 2023 and has continued helping
			lead the negotiation; in March 2026 the parties announced a
			framework for a binding agreement covering a long-term community
			investment fund, economic development lending, educational
			programming, and environmental stewardship.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4">
			Over the same period he consulted for Salmon Valley Stewardship in
			Salmon, Idaho, advising on the structure and mechanisms of a
			potential community benefits agreement with the developer of a
			nearby cobalt mine.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4">
			Jackson joined Montana State University in January 2023. He holds
			a B.S. (2017) and an M.S. (2020) in Earth Sciences from MSU, where
			his master's research examined the local costs and benefits of
			modern mineral mines and the role of non-regulatory agreements. He
			has spoken to this work as an invited panelist at the National
			Academies of Sciences, Engineering, and Medicine (2024) and in a
			U.S. Department of Energy / U.S. Energy Association webinar on
			community benefits plans (2023).
		</p>
		{/* 2026-08-13, his calls: river photo placement A (closes Background,
		    breaking the page's longest text run); NO caption and NO credit
		    line — offered both, he declined both. The file's own metadata
		    dates it 11 Nov 2025 on the Yellowstone in Paradise Valley; that
		    stays out of the rendered page at his choice. Alt text stays.
		    Original in OneDrive\Pictures\Portfolio (IMG_6248.HEIC);
		    _staging source is the HEIC converted to JPEG with EXIF (incl.
		    GPS) dropped, derivative verified zero EXIF entries. */}
		<figure class="mt-6">
			<Image
				src={yellowstone}
				alt="Jackson Rose at the oars of a drift boat on a calm river in late fall, smiling, with bare cottonwoods and a gray sky behind him."
				widths={[480, 800, 1200]}
				sizes="(min-width: 640px) 45rem, calc(100vw - 3rem)"
				class="w-full"
			/>
		</figure>
	</section>

	{/*
		Funded research. Sponsors and substance only: no amounts, no counts,
		no active/inactive status, all for the reasons in the header comment.
		The FAA award sits in its own sentence because it is the one piece of
		unmanned-aircraft work here, and grouping it with the policy awards
		would misrepresent what the first three are.
	*/}
	<section class="mt-12 border-t border-line pt-8">
		<h2 class="text-3xl">Funded research</h2>
		<p class="mt-4">
			At Montana State, Jackson is co-principal investigator on sponsored
			research funded by the National Science Foundation, the U.S.
			Department of Energy, and the U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service. The
			work spans training and resources for communities negotiating
			benefits agreements around new mines, the Department of Energy's
			CORE-CM critical minerals initiative, and the expansion of a public
			river conditions tool used for drought management across the
			northern Rockies and plains. He also holds an award under the FAA's
			ASSURE center for unmanned aircraft safety training.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4">
			Awards are listed with sponsors and periods on the{' '}
			<a href="/cv">CV page</a>.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="mt-12 border-t border-line pt-8">
		<h2 class="text-3xl">Teaching</h2>
		<p class="mt-4">
			Jackson teaches the advanced and capstone courses in the
			Department of Earth Sciences' three-course GIS sequence, along
			with the graduate companion course.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4">
			<strong>GPHY 384 — Advanced Geographic Information Science and
			Spatial Analysis.</strong> The second course in the sequence:
			spatial data models, analytical methods, error and uncertainty,
			database design, and Python in Jupyter notebooks, with weekly labs
			in both proprietary and open-source software.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4">
			<strong>GPHY 504 — GIS Research Fundamentals.</strong> The
			graduate companion, co-convened with 384 and designed for graduate
			students new to GIS. It adds a weekly recitation where students
			lead discussions of spatial-science literature from their own
			fields, and a project tied to each student's research program.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4">
			<strong>GPHY 484R — Applied GIS and Spatial Analysis.</strong> The
			capstone. One half is quantitative and statistical analysis of
			geospatial data; the other is a semester-long team project for a
			real client, ending in professional deliverables and each
			student's own portfolio.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="mt-12 border-t border-line pt-8">
		<h2 class="text-3xl">CV</h2>
		<p class="mt-4">
			{/* Explicit {' '} — the line break before the link was being
			    collapsed, rendering "is on theCV page". Found 2026-07-30. */}
			{/* 2026-08-13, flags 5 + his catch: this read "A selected CV —
			    appointments, ... — is on the CV page", which said "CV" three
			    times counting the h2 above it. Rewritten so the list leads and
			    "CV" is said once. "Selected" comes off here; the /cv page's own
			    first line says "Selected CV, updated <month>". */}
			Appointments, education, certifications, publications,
			presentations, and service are on the{' '}
			<a href="/cv">CV page</a>.
		</p>
	</section>

	{/*
	  Consulting. The RCRC paragraph is Jackson's own phrasing ("partner",
	  "consulting arm"), reconfirmed 2026-07-28 — do not rewrite it.

	  AVAILABILITY paragraph added 2026-07-30, when he decided the site
	  should say he is for hire (it previously described the affiliation
	  without ever stating he takes work or how to start). Placement is his:
	  Home and About. Voice matches the Home line he picked, and the service
	  list is fuller here because About is not bound by Home's rule against
	  naming UAS and LiDAR. The three areas are the ones he selected as work
	  he wants more of: community and policy consulting, geospatial
	  production, and tool building. Research partnerships were on the menu
	  and he did NOT select them — do not add them back.

	  SCOPE, his words the same day: "This portfolio site is not intended to
	  drive work to the GCF." No page routes consulting inquiries to the
	  Geospatial Core Facility, and the address here is the RCRC one.
	*/}
	<section class="mt-12 border-t border-line pt-8">
		<h2 class="text-3xl">Consulting</h2>
		<p class="mt-4">
			Jackson is a partner with <a href="https://rcrcgroup.org">Resources
			and Communities Research &amp; Consulting</a> (RCRC), the
			consulting arm of the Resources and Communities Research Group,
			delivering policy-relevant work on community, natural resource,
			and development challenges for governments, community
			organizations, and coalitions across the American West.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4">
			He is available for consulting work in three areas: community
			benefits agreements and the fiscal analysis that supports them,
			including negotiation support for community organizations and
			local governments; geospatial collection and analysis, from UAS
			and LiDAR survey through spatial analysis and cartography; and
			custom tools that put those numbers in front of the people who
			need them.
		</p>
		{
			site.emailConsulting && (
				<p class="mt-4">
					Inquiries:{' '}
					<a href={`mailto:${site.emailConsulting}`}>
						{site.emailConsulting}
					</a>
					.
				</p>
			)
		}
	</section>
</Base>
